        <!-- Article Body -->
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Reactive power compensation is the part of power quality that shows up on two bills at once: the utility's power factor penalty and the losses inside your own plant. As loads get faster — EV chargers, welding lines, rapidly switching drives — a switched capacitor bank stops tracking them. This article covers how a static var generator (SVG) built on a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">3-level IGBT inverter</strong> works, how to size a modular SVG, where it fails, and how it pairs with an active harmonic filter (AHF) on the same bus.</p>
<h2 id="what-reactive-power-compensation-actually-does" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">What Reactive Power Compensation Actually Does</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Reactive current is the component of load current that is 90° out of phase with voltage. It does no useful work, but it must be generated and carried, so it loads transformers, cables, and generators with current that produces heat and voltage drop without producing torque.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The displacement power factor is the cosine of the angle between the fundamental voltage and current:</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">PF = P / S = cos φ</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A plant at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">PF 0.80</strong> draws 1.25 kVA for every 1 kW of real power. Correcting to <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">0.99</strong> cuts that to about 1.01 kVA — roughly a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">19% reduction in line current</strong> at the same load, and with it a matching drop in I²R losses.</p>
<h2 id="why-fixed-capacitors-fall-short" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why Fixed Capacitors Fall Short</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A capacitor bank delivers reactive power proportional to the square of the voltage and fixed in step size. It cannot follow a load that swings within a cycle, and it cannot deliver leading current in steps smaller than one can.</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Step granularity</strong> — a 50 kvar step on a 200 kvar bank means the correction jumps in 25% blocks; PF hunts around the target instead of holding it.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Switching delay</strong> — contactor-switched stages respond in seconds to tens of seconds, far slower than an arc furnace or a welder.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Resonance risk</strong> — a capacitor bank interacts with transformer and line impedance to create a parallel resonance, typically in the 5th to 13th harmonic range, that amplifies existing distortion.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">No per-phase control</strong> — a three-phase bank corrects the average, not the unbalanced phase.</li></ul>
<h2 id="how-a-3-level-igbt-inverter-generates-reactive-current" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How a 3-Level IGBT Inverter Generates Reactive Current</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A static var generator is a voltage-source converter that synthesizes current rather than switching capacitance. It measures load current, computes the reactive component, and injects or absorbs exactly that current at the point of connection.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The control reference is straightforward:</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">i_c* = i_L(reactive) - i_s(reactive)</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">3-level IGBT inverter</strong> produces three voltage levels per phase leg (positive, neutral, negative) instead of two. The output waveform steps in smaller increments, so the current tracks the reference with lower ripple and lower harmonic content. That is why compensation stays smooth rather than jittery when the load changes abruptly.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Response is sub-cycle — <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">&lt; 1 ms</strong> — and regulation is continuous, typically held within <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">±1%</strong> of the reactive current setpoint. Power factor is held at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">0.99+</strong> across the load range rather than at one operating point.</p>
<h2 id="modular-svg-sizing-and-selection-math" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Modular SVG: Sizing and Selection Math</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Modularity is the practical answer to two problems: sizing accuracy and growth. A modular SVG is built from identical power modules that parallel at the DC link and share a controller, so capacity is added in defined blocks.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Worked example, stated assumptions:</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li>Measured load: <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">800 kW</strong> at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">PF 0.82</strong>, three-shift operation.</li><li>Target: <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">PF 0.98</strong> at the PCC.</li><li>Required reactive power: Q = P × (tan φ1 - tan φ2) = 800 × (0.698 - 0.203) ≈ <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">396 kvar</strong>.</li></ul>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Select <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">400 kvar</strong> of SVG capacity in modules (for example, eight 50 kvar modules), then check two constraints:</p>
<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Headroom</strong> — leave <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">15-25%</strong> margin for added load; 400 kvar covers the current case but not a future 100 kW expansion.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Transformer loading</strong> — confirm the SVG's own losses and the corrected current stay within the transformer's kVA rating at worst-case ambient.</li></ol>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A modular frame lets you commission 250 kvar now and add modules later without replacing the cabinet or the controller.</p>
<h2 id="what-poor-compensation-costs" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">What Poor Compensation Costs</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Utility penalties</strong> — most industrial tariffs apply a PF penalty below <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">0.90</strong> and sometimes a reactive energy charge in kvarh.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Line and transformer losses</strong> — losses scale with current squared; the 19% current reduction above cuts I²R losses by roughly a third.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Derated capacity</strong> — reactive current occupies transformer and generator capacity that could carry real load.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Voltage drop and flicker</strong> — reactive swings produce voltage fluctuations that disturb other loads on the same feeder.</li></ul>
<h2 id="voltage-flicker-and-load-balancing" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Voltage Flicker and Load Balancing</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Flicker is the visible symptom of fast reactive swings. A welder or arc furnace draws bursts of reactive current that modulate the bus voltage; the SVG counter-injects within a cycle, holding voltage steady. IEC 61000-3-3 and IEC 61000-3-11 define the flicker limits that apply at the point of common coupling, and IEEE 519 governs the harmonic side.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The same converter rebalances phases. Because each phase leg is controlled independently, the SVG can inject different reactive current per phase and cancel the negative-sequence component that a three-phase capacitor bank cannot touch.</p>
<h2 id="ahf-and-svg-on-the-same-bus" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">AHF and SVG on the Same Bus</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Nonlinear loads — rectifiers, VFDs, LED drivers, EV chargers — add harmonic current on top of the reactive problem. An active harmonic filter measures load current and synthesizes the inverse of the harmonic content:</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">i_c* = i_L - i_s(fundamental)</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A bridge switching at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">15-20 kHz</strong> tracks within <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">&lt; 1 ms</strong> and pulls <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">THDi from 30%+ down to &lt; 5%</strong>, meeting IEEE 519 at the PCC. Where both problems exist, a combined CHITEK AHF + CHITEK SVG platform shares the DC link and the current sensors, which avoids the interaction between two independently tuned devices.</p>
<h2 id="igbt-vs-sic-where-the-topology-is-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">IGBT vs SiC: Where the Topology Is Heading</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Conventional LV SVG and AHF units use silicon IGBTs capped near <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">150 °C</strong> junction temperature, switching typically below <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">20 kHz</strong>. SiC MOSFETs raise the junction limit to about <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">200 °C</strong>, offer roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">10×</strong> the breakdown field, switch at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">2-3×</strong> the frequency, and cut switching loss by about <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">50%</strong>. Higher switching frequency widens control bandwidth and shrinks the output filter, which is the direction CHITEK's LV AHF + SVG platform is built on.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6"><table class="w-full border-collapse"><thead><tr class="bg-gray-50"><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">Parameter</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">2-level IGBT</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">3-level IGBT</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">SiC 3-level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Output levels per leg</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">2</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">3</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">3</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Typical switching frequency</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">&lt; 10 kHz</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">10-20 kHz</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">20-40 kHz</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Output ripple</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Higher</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Lower</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Lowest</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Switching loss</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Baseline</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Similar</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~50% lower</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Junction limit</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~150 °C</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~150 °C</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~200 °C</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="engineering-deployment-checklist" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Engineering Deployment Checklist</h2>
<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li>Measure phase voltages, currents, PF, and THDi at the PCC over a full duty cycle — including the worst 15-minute window, not an average shift.</li><li>Compute required kvar from the measured P and the target PF; state the target explicitly.</li><li>Place the SVG close to the offending load, not at the main incomer, if the goal is flicker or phase balancing.</li><li>Audit existing capacitor banks for resonance and mistuning before commissioning; a detuned reactor changes the tuning point.</li><li>Verify CT ratio, polarity, and mounting direction — reversed CTs are the most common commissioning fault on active compensators.</li><li>Check weak-grid margin and size the converter for the worst-case voltage dip, not nominal.</li><li>After commissioning, log PF, THDi, and voltage THD for a week and compare against IEEE 519 and the contract limits.</li></ol>
<h2 id="typical-application-profiles" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Typical Application Profiles</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">EV charging hubs</strong> — clusters of single-phase chargers unbalance the bus and swing reactive demand; a modular SVG rebalances per phase and holds PF.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Welding and arc-furnace plants</strong> — violent, fast reactive swings; the SVG stabilizes voltage and suppresses flicker.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">C&amp;I plants with mixed single/three-phase load</strong> — a combined AHF + SVG unit removes harmonic distortion and cuts PF penalties at once.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Renewable and storage interconnections</strong> — inverters must supply reactive current on command; an SVG provides that function independent of generation.</li></ul>
<h2 id="bottom-line" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Bottom Line</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Reactive power compensation is a dynamics problem: fixed capacitors correct an average, while a 3-level IGBT static var generator corrects the waveform cycle by cycle. A modular SVG lets you size to the measured load, add capacity as the plant grows, and pair with an active harmonic filter where distortion is present. CHITEK's LV AHF + SVG platforms are specified from exactly this measurement-first discipline.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">What is your measured power factor at the PCC — and how much does it swing between light and peak load?</p>
